# A finite, driven unitary reservoir for the boundary clock's reset endpoint

Status: new conditional construction, not an accepted theorem or retained audit.
This is a guided follow-up to the supplied Markov-reset interface. The proof
below does not assume that interface is microscopically realized, and does not
approximate its whole dissipative trajectory. It replaces its reset endpoint by
parallel, finite-duration SWAP controls on a specified finite reservoir, while
the complete original installed generator remains active. Fresh purity,
vacancies, addressable interactions, an external schedule and precision remain
supplied. A finite closed system plus these driven controls is not an autonomous
controller or a native bath.

## 1. Source, state and Hamiltonian contract

The exact boundary source is b3b6cb24b1f89396921fdcae7aea2394b4d4d7338bda0d1e1877a1a997a78be1,
archived as `sources/boundary_derivation.md`. Its complete argument and literal
geometry were read, with unchanged prior source-exposed coverage reused. The
reset source 95fbc8dddb92e732e76327546eeb2ec89ce0a673939c8135c5f160c72fa03825
was read completely and archived as `sources/reset_derivation.md`. At first
exposure it was provisional. A later parent message reported conditional source
acceptance; this status supplies no microscopic-reservoir result. No separate
reset assessment was read. Exact roles, chronology and reading limits appear in
`EXPOSURE.md` and `SOURCE_PINS.json`.

Let D be the complete original finite old carrier, C the boundary clock and its
eight mediators, and R any passive reference. The initial normalized density
operator sigma_DCR is arbitrary: it may have every excitation sector of C,
old-clock entanglement, and classical or quantum correlations with R. No
one-head, product, old-eigenstate or old-code premise is imposed on this input.
Let K be the unchanged finite Hermitian generator on D, with every original
coefficient, scalar and reserve retained. K may fail to commute with the old
Paulis in the clock apparatus. Fix a real center a and a certified finite
h >= ||K-aI||. This center changes only estimates, not the installed operator.

Write

    H0 = K + H_app,       N_C = sum_(u in C) n_u.                 (1)

The exact boundary supplies the following whole-hard-core-space statements:

    H_app |vac_C> = 0 on every old vector,
    [H0,N_C] = 0,
    delta N_C <= H_app <= B I,     ||H_app|| = B,    delta > 0.   (2)

It also supplies, for the actual entry e=v0, the exact bare-entry compressions

    J_e* H0 J_e = K + alpha0 I,
    J_e* H0^2 J_e = (K+alpha0 I)^2 + q I,
    q = 2 Lambda |z0|^2,
    G_e := ||H_app J_e|| = sqrt(alpha0^2+q).                    (3)

These are full old-input identities; the actual evolution is never projected
onto a one-excitation subspace in this proof. Equation (2), rather than a
one-particle norm used as a full-space norm, controls the first pulse.

For precision and reconstruction the unchanged literal coefficients are:

    L >= 8, Omega > 0, Lambda > 0,
    J_j = Omega sqrt((j+1)(L-j)), 0 <= j < L,
    kappa_j = J_j for j=0,1,
    P0=X_(q0), P1=Z_(q0), phi0=-pi/4, phi1=pi/4,
    g_j=sqrt(kappa_j),
    z_j=sqrt(kappa_j)[-(209/120)cos(phi_j)+i(209/32)sin(phi_j)],
    s_(j,l)=448 kappa_j/209,  s_(j,r)=480|z_j|^2/209,
    c=Omega(L+1),  alpha_v=c+s_v,
    s_(v0)=s_(0,r), s_(v1)=s_(0,l)+s_(1,l),
    s_(v2)=s_(1,r), s_(vj)=0 for j>=3,
    A=sum_heads alpha_v=(L+1)c+sum_(j=0,1)(s_(j,l)+s_(j,r)),
    B=A+8Lambda,  alpha0=c+s_(0,r).                             (4)

With a_u=|0><1|_u, n_u=a_u* a_u, exchange
T_uv=a_u* a_v+a_v* a_u, and B_uv(z)=z a_u* a_v+conj(z)a_v* a_u,
the exact apparatus in (1) is

    L_j=sum_(u in four mediators j)n_u
          +(P_j/4)(n_b-n_b')-(T_ab+T_a'b')/4,
    V_j=g_j(T_al+T_a'l)+B_br(z_j)+B_b'r(conj(z_j)),
    H_app=Lambda(L0+L1)+sqrt(Lambda)(V0+V1)
          +sum_heads alpha_v n_v +sum_(j=2)^(L-1)J_j T_(v_j,v_(j+1)). (5)

The strict lower bound can be taken as

    f^2=max(2|z0|^2, 2(kappa0+kappa1), 2|z1|^2),
    delta=min(Omega,Lambda/2)/(1+2f/sqrt(Lambda))^2.              (6)

The boundary's whole-space Schur/lift argument, positivity decomposition and
entry calculation are the explicit mathematical import. This note is not a
new independent review of those already read statements. The subsequent reset
construction uses (1)-(3) directly. It does not use the Markov source's decay,
gap, energy-loss, or numerical-check conclusions.

## 2. Literal additional vacancies and selected edges

Retain the old occupied chain q_l=(l,0,0), 0<=l<n_old, n_old>=2, and every old
term, including the bond q0-q1. The boundary's C consists of n=L+9 qubits:

    v0=(0,1,1), v1=(-1,0,0),
    v_j=(0,1-j,-1), 2<=j<=L,
    a0=(-1,1,0), b0=(0,1,0), a0'=(-1,0,1), b0'=(0,0,1),
    a1=(-1,-1,0), b1=(0,-1,0), a1'=(-1,0,-1), b1'=(0,0,-1).

Both cells have local left head v1; the right heads are respectively v0 and v2.
In particular v0 is a right port, which is why (3) contains z0, not g0. The
boundary already uses five of q0's six neighbours; the remaining neighbour is
the retained old q1. Attaching a new reader or reset ancilla directly at q0 is
not assumed.

Supply one additional reservoir qubit e(u) for each u in C, at these coordinates:

| Clock or mediator u | Fresh reservoir e(u) |
| --- | --- |
| v0 | (0,2,1) |
| v1 | (-2,0,0) |
| v_j, 2<=j<=L | (1,1-j,-1) |
| a0 | (-2,1,0) |
| b0 | (1,1,0) |
| a0' | (-2,0,1) |
| b0' | (1,0,1) |
| a1 | (-2,-1,0) |
| b1 | (1,-1,0) |
| a1' | (-2,0,-1) |
| b1' | (1,0,-1) |

Every listed pair has ordinary L1 distance one. The reservoir sites are
distinct: its tail has x=1,z=-1,y<=-1; its remaining x=1,z=-1 site has y=0;
the other finite rows have different x, y or z. None is a C site or an old
chain site. This proves disjointness for every L>=8, rather than extrapolating
from a sampled layout. Additional old factors, if present, must avoid these
sites as well as C. That is an additional vacancy premise; the boundary source
does not guarantee it for an arbitrary occupied substrate.

There are n new pair edges, no routing paths, no transit factors and no
periodic shortcut. All selected pair edges have disjoint endpoints. Including
the boundary clock, the additions use 2(L+9) qubits and (L+14)+(L+9)=2L+23
selected edges. For the old chain alone the entire layout lies in the ordinary
box

    -2<=x<=max(n_old-1,1),  1-L<=y<=2,  -1<=z<=1.              (7)

Physical positions do not themselves supply interactions. We stipulate the
specified selected couplings and absence of unlisted reservoir couplings, with
any deviations priced below. The complete apparatus is onsite/NN two-body if
the old K already has that support, as in the old-chain premise. For a more
general K, only the added terms are asserted to have that support.
In particular several reservoir sites neighbour q1. The absence of an
unlisted reservoir-q1 coupling is an engineering premise, not a consequence
of distance or of the old K being retained.

The reservoir E has engineered free Hamiltonian H_E=0 and initial state
|vac_E><vac_E| exactly, uncorrelated with sigma_DCR. Thus the joint ready input is
sigma_DCR tensor |vac_E><vac_E|. This is a finite pure-memory and isolation
premise, not a result of cooling, a proof of native degeneracy, or a claim of
zero thermodynamic preparation work. A global ready-state error is priced in
Section 8. We keep E among the outputs.

## 3. Explicit positive two-qubit pulse and full retained target

Let S_u denote the full qubit SWAP of u and e(u), not the excitation exchange
T_uv in (5). The selected SWAPs commute because the pairs are disjoint.
S_u*=S_u, S_u^2=I, and I-S_u is positive with norm 2. In Pauli form,

    S_u=(I+X_u X_e+Y_u Y_e+Z_u Z_e)/2,
    I-S_u=(I-X_u X_e-Y_u Y_e-Z_u Z_e)/2.                      (8)

Choose a finite deterministic duration t_s>0 and the nonnegative smooth pulse

    gamma(s)=(pi/t_s) sin^2(pi s/t_s), 0<=s<=t_s,
    G_s(s)=gamma(s) sum_(u in C)(I-S_u),
    H_actual(s)=H0+G_s(s),       H_E=0.                       (9)

The envelope and its first derivative vanish at the endpoints. Its area is
pi/2. On the symmetric/antisymmetric pair spaces,

    exp[-i(pi/2)(I-S_u)]=S_u                                (10)

exactly, including its deterministic common phase. Consequently the comparison
propagator generated by K+G_s, with H_app omitted only in the mathematical
comparison, has endpoint

    U_cmp(t_s)=exp(-it_s K) product_u S_u.                    (11)

There is no physical switch-off of H_app and no -K or -H_app control. K commutes
with the supplied G_s because they act on different factors; it need not
commute with H_app.

Let J append the ready E vacuum. Define T to relabel the entire original C
register as E. The exact comparison isometry is

    V_s=exp(-it_s K) product_u S_u J,
    V_s |psi>_DCR=|vac_C> tensor
                  (exp(-it_s K) tensor I_ER) T|psi>_DCR.     (12)

Tensor-factor order in (12) is only notational. All original D-C-R correlations
are retained as D-E-R correlations. E is generally neither vacuum nor
uncorrelated. In particular the full target cannot be replaced by its old
marginal times a fixed pure reservoir. The reduced reset target follows only
after the mathematical partial trace over E:

    Tr_E(V_s sigma V_s*) = |vac_C><vac_C| tensor
               exp(-it_s K) sigma_DR exp(it_s K).            (13)

Tracing E is a statement about a reduced density operator, not a physical
collapse, an extra reset, or an unpriced classical instrument. No outcome is
postselected and no branch is discarded in (12).

## 4. Full-space comparison with the installed drift active

Write U_s for the actual unitary from (9). The variation-of-constants identity
gives, on the entire input domain before tensoring R,

    (U_s(t_s)-U_cmp(t_s))J
      =-i integral_0^t_s U_s(t_s,s) H_app U_cmp(s)J ds.

Every factor outside H_app is unitary or isometric and (2) is a full-space
bound. Therefore

    ||(U_s(t_s)-U_cmp(t_s))J|| <= B t_s.                     (14)

This estimate is insensitive to noncommutation of K and H_app. It compares
against the actual old free evolution, so there is no dropped ||K||t_s term.
There is also no projection that excludes sectors reached by either pulse.
Purifying any mixed sigma_DCR and using the isometry inequality yields

    ||U_s J sigma J* U_s* - V_s sigma V_s*||_1
                 <= min(2, 2 B t_s).                      (15)

The same bound holds with every passive reference, with E retained, and hence
after any partial trace. This is a prepared-map bound on the sole ready E
domain and all clock inputs. It is not a diamond comparison on arbitrary dirty
reservoir inputs.

There is a useful additional distinction from Markov damping. The nominal
first pulse preserves N_C+N_E, and the exact ideal endpoint places all original
C excitation information into E. Nevertheless (9) need not produce a monotone
clock population or monotone installed energy along the actual trajectory.
Neither the reset source's exponential N_C law nor its dissipative energy
balance is claimed for (9). Equation (15) is solely an endpoint statement.

## 5. Finite head preparation and the later word

After (9) turns off, prepare the actual entry v0 with a second finite onsite
pulse of duration t_p>0,

    omega_p(s)=(pi/t_p) sin^2(pi s/t_p),
    G_p(s)=omega_p(s)(I-X_v0),
    H_actual=H0+G_p(s).                                    (16)

Its area is pi/2, and exp[-i(pi/2)(I-X_v0)]=X_v0 exactly. This is the explicit
entry pulse appearing in the reset interface, here checked directly. Let J_vac
append C vacuum and regard E together with R as a passive reference. During
the comparison evolution generated by K+G_p, the C factor is a superposition
of vacuum and the bare v0 state. H_app kills the vacuum, and (3) bounds its
action on the entry component by G_e. A second Duhamel identity therefore gives

    ||(U_p-U_(K+G_p)) J_vac|| <= G_e t_p.                   (17)

The statement is uniform over arbitrary D-E-R correlations. Applying actual
U_p to the error in (14) preserves its norm. Combining the two stages, set

    t0=t_s+t_p,  d=B t_s+G_e t_p,
    V_0 |psi>_DCR = |entry_C> tensor exp(-it0 K) T|psi>_DCR.

Then the complete retained-output preparation satisfies

    ||U_p U_s J-V_0|| <= d,
    ||rho_actual-DCE R - V_0 sigma V_0*||_1 <= min(2,2d).    (18)

Here `rho_actual-DCE R` denotes the actual density operator on D,C,E,R, not a
subtraction. For desired positive allowances eps_s and eps_p, a sufficient
choice is

    t_s <= eps_s/(2B),  t_p <= eps_p/(2G_e),
    eps_out=eps_s+eps_p.                                   (19)

The literal positive parameters make B,G_e strictly positive. All choices are
finite given effective finite input data and certified upper bounds. Larger
certified B or G_e may be used in (19). No limiting pulse or instantaneous
operation is needed. A smaller allowed time requires the larger strength paid
in Section 6; a fixed insufficient control cap is not bypassed.

The displayed schedule has no idle gap between its two pulses. An explicitly
declared gap after an ideal reset vacuum may be included in t0: that ideal
state evolves exactly under K and the existing error is preserved by common
unitary evolution. General gaps, faults, and a mismatch between actual and
nominal elapsed time require the bounds in Section 8.

For a subsequent use of the unchanged boundary word, E is simply an enlarged
passive reference. Its exact reference-complete boundary estimate may be
applied to the ideal entry in (18), then the preparation error added by
contraction. The time-indexed old target is

    exp(-it K) W exp(-it0 K),
    W=exp(-i pi Z_q0/4) exp(-i pi X_q0/4),                  (20)

acting on T sigma T*, with the complete moving clock factor supplied by the
boundary theorem. It is not exp[-i(t+t0)K]W for a noncommuting K. Reset and
preparation elapsed time have not been deleted. No stationary clock, permanent
record, completed physical reader, or stopping operation follows from (20).

## 6. Full-space positivity, support, scalar and control price

The first added pulse is positive on the complete qubit space. Its exact norm
is 2n gamma(s), since all disjoint pairs can be singlets simultaneously. The
installed H_app remains positive, so H_app+G_s is positive, as is H_app+G_p.
This does not make an arbitrary signed K+H_app+G positive. If K>=0 it is
positive. Otherwise a reserve already included in K may establish positivity;
no reserve is silently appended here. Adding a new reserve would change the
specified generator and must be declared with its mean, scalar, phase and
timing costs.

One can check the apparatus's transient whole-space gap without using it as a
preparation mechanism. For gamma>0 the two-qubit calculation gives

    delta n_u+gamma(I-S_u) >= d_gamma(n_u+n_e),
    d_gamma=(delta+2gamma-sqrt(delta^2+4gamma^2))/2>0.          (21)

On the one-excitation pair block the two eigenvalues are those of
[[delta+gamma,-gamma],[-gamma,gamma]]. On |11> the left value is delta and
2d_gamma<=delta; |00> has value zero. Summing (21) and using (2) proves a
strict transient apparatus gap above the all-C/E vacuum. At gamma=0 the
degenerate free E memory makes this gap zero. No gap of the complete arbitrary
K generator or gap-based state preparation is inferred.

Under the whole-term operator-norm convention, each selected pair has added
load 2gamma(s), peaking at 2pi/t_s on each of its two sites. No old site has a
new reset edge. The boundary's original loads, including Lambda at q0 and its
unchanged K load, remain installed. Entry adds at most 2pi/t_p at v0. If the
Pauli dictionary is used instead, (8) has three two-site coefficients of
magnitude gamma/2 and a scalar gamma/2 per pair; it is a different accounting
dictionary, not an alternative full-term norm.

Exact pulse resources are

| Quantity | Parallel reservoir pulse | Entry pulse |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Duration | t_s | t_p |
| Peak global control norm | 2n pi/t_s | 2pi/t_p |
| Integrated control norm | n pi | pi |
| Peak derivative norm | 2n pi^2/t_s^2 | 2pi^2/t_p^2 |
| Integral derivative norm | 4n pi/t_s | 4pi/t_p |
| Pauli-identity coefficient | n gamma(s)/2 | omega_p(s) |
| Integrated Pauli-identity coefficient | n pi/4 | pi/2 |

The exact endpoint identity (10) retains all scalar phases; replacing I-S by
-S without tracking its phase changes the isometry by a deterministic common
phase, though not its channel. The original Pauli scalar B/2 of H_app and
every scalar/reserve in K are retained. The cancellations making H_app vanish
on vacuum do not authorize deleting that scalar from the installed operator.

In the boundary's prescribed fixed-positive-window family, with its stated
nondegenerate fixed constants and its specified lower choices, one has

    n=O(e^-2), B=O(e^-7), alpha0=O(e^-2),
    q=O(e^-8), G_e=O(e^-4).

Choosing eps_s and eps_p proportional to e in (19) gives sufficient pulse peak
prices O(e^-8) per SWAP pair, O(e^-10) globally for their simultaneous sum,
and O(e^-5) for entry; the explicit chosen times are eps_s/(2B) and
eps_p/(2G_e). These are sufficient resource prices of this construction, not
lower bounds, optimality, or bounds for arbitrary larger parent parameters.
Degenerate asymptotic domains use the finite formulas (4), (19) and the table.
No common fixed cap, efficient cooling, or hardware-scale viability is proved.
These displayed powers target trace accuracy; fixed energy-moment allowances
also require the potentially much smaller trace error in (26).

## 7. Mean, variance, covariance, work and finite information capacity

For any initial sigma_DCR the same installed generator has exact moments

    mu_initial = Tr(K sigma_D)+Tr(H_app sigma_DC),
    Var_initial(H0)=Var_sigma(K)+Var_sigma(H_app)
       +Tr sigma {K-mu_K I,H_app-mu_app I}.                  (22)

The symmetrized covariance in (22) need not vanish: the old and clock factors
are allowed to be correlated, and K and H_app need not commute. The free
reservoir H_E=0 has zero specified energy contribution, but its supplied pure
state is not thermodynamically free by implication.

At any intermediate time, with G equal to the active pulse, the actual total
generator is H_tot=H0+G. Its exact variance is

    Var(H_tot)=Var(H0)+Var(G)
                  +Tr rho {H0-mu_0 I,G-mu_G I}.             (23)

No covariance is dropped. Conservative full-space instantaneous allowances are

    beta_s(s)=h+B+2n gamma(s),  beta_p(s)=h+B+2omega_p(s),
    |Tr(H_tot rho)-a| <= beta_(s or p)(s),
    Tr[(H_tot-aI)^2 rho] <= beta_(s or p)(s)^2,
    Var_rho(H_tot) <= beta_(s or p)(s)^2.                   (24)

Thus both the installed and driven energy spreads are paid. A physical
identification of these engineered generators with measured energy remains a
separate premise, not supplied by positivity alone.

Since the controls vanish at each stage's endpoints, closed unitary evolution
obeys the exact work identity

    Delta Tr(H0 rho) = integral Tr(dot G(s) rho(s)) ds.

In particular the absolute changes are bounded by 4n pi/t_s and 4pi/t_p,
respectively. They need not be energy losses. The Markov source's formula
-r(t)Tr(H_app rho), its jump interpretation and its dissipative Q bounds do
not apply. The external controller's own Hamiltonian, clock preparation and
energy storage have not been microscopically constructed; these norm/work
allowances price the supplied driven system, not an omitted autonomous driver.

At the ideal reset endpoint, the H0 moments are precisely the old K moments
of sigma_D. At the ideal final entry endpoint of (18),

    mu_* = Tr(K sigma_D)+alpha0,
    v_* = Var_(sigma_D)(K)+q.                              (25)

Although E retains correlations, these scalar formulas follow from (3) and
free K aging, not from a product assumption on initial sigma. If the actual
complete output differs in trace norm by eps, let beta=h+B. On that SAME
installed H0, with controls now off,

    |mu_actual-mu_*| <= beta eps,
    |Tr[(H0-aI)^2(rho_actual-rho_*)]| <= beta^2 eps,
    |Var_actual(H0)-v_*| <= 3 beta^2 eps.                   (26)

The last inequality adds beta^2 eps for the second moment and 2beta^2 eps for
the squared centered means. For either passive record Q=R or Q=ER, duality
also gives, j=1,2,

    ||Tr_DC[((H0-aI)^j tensor I_Q)(rho_actual-rho_*)]||_1
                         <= beta^j eps,                  (27)

with E traced as well when Q=R. Equation (27) is a linear reference-valued
mathematical record, not a nonlinear conditional variance or an energy reader.
Raw uncentered moments require ||H0|| and ||H0||^2, or the explicitly restored
a-dependent terms. A small trace error has not made high-energy accuracy
free. For desired scalar mean and variance allowances E_mu,E_v>0, it suffices
to impose additionally eps<=min(E_mu/beta,E_v/(3beta^2)).

The exact ideal reset stores the entire n-qubit C state in n reservoir qubits,
including its entanglement with D/R. For orthogonal clock basis inputs with
the same old state, the ideal E outputs are orthogonal. An exactly unitary
replacement with a pure initial environment therefore needs at least 2^n
environment dimensions. This construction supplies that dimension exactly.
For an independent maximally mixed C it transfers n log 2 entropy; it does
not destroy information. The dimension statement is for exact replacement,
not a new approximate lower bound for (15).

E generally cannot be reused as a clean reservoir after one use. Undoing its
information transfer restores the corresponding clock information; discarding
E as a subsystem does not prepare it again. Repeated uses need fresh pure
memory or a separately priced restoration mechanism. Engineered H_E=0 and
zero post-pulse couplings allow finite retained storage within this stipulated
model, but physical memory noise and later reading require their own contract.

## 8. Calibration, actual time and remaining physical operations

All nominal evolution above is on the complete D,C,E space. If the joint ready
input is within eta_ready in trace norm of sigma_DCR tensor vacuum_E, and the
actual full Hermitian generator has an integrable perturbation F(s), then

    eps_total <= min(2, eta_ready+2B t_s+2G_e t_p
                                      +2 integral ||F(s)|| ds). (28)

This follows from initial-state contraction and unitary Duhamel. F may break
excitation conservation or couple an unintended sector; a small restriction
only to the vacuum or one-head subspace is not sufficient for (28). R stays
passive; adversarial couplings to an uncontrolled reference are outside that
model. A finite F bound on the actual whole space is a supplied calibration
certificate, not inferred from a few pulse samples.

For amplitude errors delta_gamma_u(s) on individual pair generators, and
delta_omega(s) on entry, a sufficient contribution is

    4 sum_u integral |delta_gamma_u(s)| ds
                          +4 integral |delta_omega(s)| ds. (29)

This counts every one of n simultaneous controls. It is compatible with the
finite peak and slope allowances in Section 6. For example, shifting the
whole reservoir envelope by d in a common fixed comparison interval, extending
it by zero outside its support, gives

    integral |gamma(s-d)-gamma(s)| ds <= (2pi/t_s)|d|,

and hence the pulse-mismatch contribution 8n pi|d|/t_s. Entry similarly costs
8pi|d|/t_p. These are conservative sufficient bounds; they do not cover a
change of total elapsed time unless that change is also included. Comparison
at a wrong endpoint time dt can separately cost up to 2||H0|| |dt| in trace
norm for post-pulse free evolution (or use the actual time-indexed target).
The full scalar is retained for phase-sensitive map statements.

A general idle gap of duration g may conservatively add 2B g when the desired
comparison retains K but omits H_app; a gap on the ideal reset vacuum is the
special exact case explained after (19). No undefined gap is hidden in t0.
Unwanted reservoir free terms, residual memory couplings, imperfect switching,
finite control precision and a finite later storage interval are examples for
which a declared integral in (28) is required. If a fixed storage disturbance
has norm bound nu for time T_store, the additional trace allowance is at most
2nu T_store relative to the stipulated decoupled model.

No terminal measurement is required for the reset/preparation endpoint. A
subsequent classical flag, physical energy measurement, nonzero-duration reader
or autonomous pulse scheduler has not been constructed by these equations.
Its actual backaction and full elapsed time cannot be replaced by a promised
instantaneous channel. The result is therefore a finite externally scheduled
unitary supplier of the prepared state, with both its environment and its
remaining physical interfaces visible.

## 9. Failed shortcuts, selected evidence and exact closure

Three tempting shortcuts fail. First, the boundary leaves no extra neighbour
at q0, so an unlisted central reset port is not available; Section 2 uses the
clock sites themselves. Second, a dirty reservoir does not reset C under
SWAP: it places that dirty input into C. Third, requiring both a reset clock
and the unchanged pure reservoir would erase arbitrary orthogonal inputs by a
unitary, which is impossible by preservation of inner products. The retained
E target in (12) is essential even when only (13) is wanted later.

A further rejected inference would transfer the Markov trajectory or its
monotone energy balance to these pulses. Only the common endpoint objective is
reproduced with the error (18). This note makes no native cooling, detailed
balance, thermalization, infinite reuse or broad no-go claim.

The independently written selected exact control in `selected_control.py`
addresses one concrete risk: a purported reset endpoint that silently loses
old/clock/reference correlations or returns dirty memory as if pure. It checks
the complete toy ready-domain SWAP isometry, a specified correlated input and
missing-SWAP/dirty-memory/common-phase mutants. It also checks the explicit
matching at one L=8 layout. Its plan, input and source are sealed before its
single execution. This finite discriminator is not a simulation of the
boundary many-body dynamics, a numerical proof of (14), or an all-L geometry
test. General geometry and the arbitrary-reference bound are the analytic
arguments above. Actual results, including any failure, belong in the report
and full preserved streams rather than being anticipated here.

The construction removes the supplied Markov-generator/bath realization
premise for this finite reset endpoint. It replaces it with n explicit fresh
pure qubits, a collision-free NN matching, degenerate isolated memory and
calibrated positive finite pulses plus an external schedule. It preserves
every old term and every clock term during those pulses. It closes neither
the native supply of purity/vacancies/controls nor autonomous scheduling,
permanent storage, a classical reader, arbitrary crowded-layout access or a
fixed-cap efficiency theorem. These are actual remaining interfaces, not
assumptions implicitly certified by the endpoint mathematics.
